    "result_text": "The full suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS). The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the city-capacity rule for `POST /api/owners`: creating an owner is now rejected with **409** when the target city already holds 50 or more owners.\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions, I mirrored the existing `EnsureUniqueHousehold` pattern:\n\n**New files:**\n- `EnsureCityHasCapacity.java` \u2014 a create-owner step that counts existing owners in the request's city and throws when it's at capacity (limit `50`, defined as a named constant).\n- `CityAtCapacityException.java` \u2014 checked exception (so it surfaces in the `throws` clause).\n- `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 maps the exception to a 409 `ProblemDetail`, reusing the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)`.\n- `officefloor/escalation/...CityAtCapacityException.yml` \u2014 wires the handler.\n\n**Reuse / structure:**\n- Added a small `SameCity.count(owners, city)` helper (single home for the \"how many owners in this city\" logic) and refactored `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` to use it, removing its duplicated counting loop. The new capacity step uses the same helper, so the case-insensitive city comparison lives in exactly one place.\n- Inserted the `ensureCityCapacity` step into `owners.POST.yml` after `ensureHousehold` and before `build`, under the same `transaction` governance as its neighbours \u2014 a rejection happens before any entity is built or persisted.\n\nThe `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` test (fill a city to 50, expect the 51st to be a 409) and the full suite pass.",
